        "response": {
          "output": "Build the evals first, but only a small set: 5-10 cases that cover the main behavior and the failure modes you already know about. Write a rough first prompt, run it against those cases, then change the prompt and the cases together.\n\nWriting the evals first forces you to define what \"good\" means before the prompt can bias you. It also gives you a baseline, so each prompt edit shows up as a measured change and not a guess.\n\nDon't build a large eval suite before there's a prompt. You can't tell which cases matter until you've seen the prompt fail.\n\nIf you want the toolkit's version of this loop, the `prompt-edd` skill covers it."
